    id: "8dd22500111456b4e91d",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Iraq’s PM tells Saudi crown prince Iraqi territory will not be used for attacks",
    summary: "Saudi Arabia's East-West oil pipeline was struck by drones last month in an attack that originated in Iraq.",
    preview: "Saudi Arabia’s East-West oil pipeline was struck by drones last month in an attack that originated in Iraq. Iraqi Prime Minister Ali Faleh al-Zaidi has assured Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman (MBS) that he will not allow Iraqi territory to be used to launch attacks against Saudi Arabia, the Iraqi News Agency (INA) has reported. Al-Zaidi arrived in Riyadh on Wednesday for his first visit to the kingdom since taking office in May. Recommended Stories list of 3 items - list 1 of 3Is Iran charging a toll to allow oil traffic through Hormuz?",
    body: "Saudi Arabia’s East-West oil pipeline was struck by drones last month in an attack that originated in Iraq.\n\nIraqi Prime Minister Ali Faleh al-Zaidi has assured Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman (MBS) that he will not allow Iraqi territory to be used to launch attacks against Saudi Arabia, the Iraqi News Agency (INA) has reported.\n\nAl-Zaidi arrived in Riyadh on Wednesday for his first visit to the kingdom since taking office in May.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Is Iran charging a toll to allow oil traffic through Hormuz?\n\n- list 2 of 3Saudi Arabia says East-West pipeline pumping 5.8 million bpd\n\n- list 3 of 3Why is US turning to Russia for diesel despite sanctions?\n\nHe also said he would not allow attacks against other regional countries to be launched from Iraq.\n\n“Iraq’s firm position is not to allow its territory to be used as a launching pad for any attacks or actions that affect the security of the kingdom and the countries of the region, and to be keen on adhering to the principles of good neighbourliness and respecting the sovereignty of Iraq and brotherly and friendly countries,” Al-Zaidi’s office said in a statement, cited by INA.\n\nSaudi Arabia’s East-West oil pipeline was struck by drones last month during the Houthis’ lightning offensive in Yemen.\n\nThe attack forced Riyadh to temporarily shut down the export route, which Saudi Arabia has used as an alternative to the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nThe pipeline, which has a maximum capacity of seven million barrels per day, has since resumed operations.\n\nSaudi Arabia said the drone attack originated in Iraq. Baghdad conducted its own investigation and concluded that the strikes were launched from Iraqi territory.",
